{
  "what_this_is": "Real end-to-end decode of Qwen3.8-27B on a pinned SparkInfer commit, through integrations/sparkinfer/. Control and candidate are the same binary, interleaved on one RTX 5090. This is the measurement sections 17, 21 and 28 make authoritative; the synthetic file beside it is not.",
  "measurement_caveats": [
    "Graphics clocks could not be pinned on this box, so absolute tok/s is not reproducible off it; the paired control/candidate deltas are.",
    "The batch-1 mode axis used 3 interleaved pairs and measured a 0.023% noise floor. Every other axis used 2 pairs, from which real_sweep.py refuses to estimate a floor at all - a two-sample peak-to-peak spread can be exactly zero. Read those spans against the 0.023% floor measured on the same workload, not against their own.",
    "Concurrency figures are aggregate continuous-batching throughput from qwen3_gguf_cb_bench, a different code path (decode_packed) from the batch-1 bench. The adapter's telemetry reports tokens_packed so a run that silently fell back to the single-sequence path can be identified rather than reported as concurrency.",
    "The 32-sequence arms of the mode screen do not resolve: two of four produced 30%+ spreads between repeats, which are the runtime falling off its batched decode path rather than a cache effect. concurrency32_capture_probe below is the dedicated isolated-run measurement that replaces them, and concurrency 32 is deliberately absent from the scored matrix."
  ],
